Producer batch 2: the two SYNTHETIC (ADR-0031 Class B) route-marker producers.
Both are name-preserving; what changes is node.id, which in both cases was not a
parseable ADR-0036 record.

make_route_symbol gains `protocol_origin`: when set, the marker is a synthetic
stand-in, so Symbol.language becomes None and the protocol moves to the typed
protocol_origin field, while the id lang-slot still names a language and the id
stays five canonical segments.

`discovery_language` is an EXPLICIT parameter, not derived from `language`. My
first cut derived it whenever protocol_origin was set, which would have flipped
grpc's discovery_language from None to "proto" -- and grpc carries an in-code
comment stating it leaves that field None on purpose, because a gRPC route is
fabricated from a service definition rather than discovered inside a host file.
Deriving it would have silently reversed a recorded decision from a factory two
packages away. The factory owns identity; it does not get to make semantic
classifications for the caller.

grpc:
  * id kind-slot was the literal `route` fossil (unregistered; kind was already
    `function`)
  * id lang-slot was the PROTOCOL `grpc`, not a language -- protocol now lives
    in protocol_origin, lang-slot names the host file's language like every
    other Class-B linker id
  * grpc:user.proto:5:RPC /x:route -> proto:user.proto:5-5:RPC /x:function
  * deletes the local _make_route_stable_id, a copy of the core function kept
    "to avoid a cross-package import" -- a reason that evaporates once this
    module imports the factory from analyze.base anyway. The core function has
    its own test (test_spec_validator_smoke).

annotation_convention:
  * id was a raw f-string with FOUR segments and no language slot, plus an
    unregistered `annotated_route` kind-slot -- an id that could not be parsed
    back into its slots at all, strictly worse than a wrong slot
  * svc.js:1:GET /users:annotated_route -> javascript:svc.js:1-1:GET /users:function
  * gains a stable_id it previously left None (null->value gap-fill: additive,
    no scheme bump, per the scheme-bump principle / WI-rihob precedent)
  * `@hg:route <method> <path>` splits on the first whitespace run, so both
    tested forms ("POST join", "POST /api/users") keep byte-identical names; a
    single-token argument reads as a method with the root path via the factory's
    existing ""->"/" normalization rather than a second naming rule

